.TH QEMU-DBUS-INPUT 1 "2026" "wsh-gst-qemu-dbus" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qemu-dbus-input \- keyboard and mouse input for QEMU's D-Bus display
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qemu-dbus-input
(\fB\-\-address\fR \fIADDRESS\fR | \fB\-\-vmid\fR \fIVMID\fR)
[\fB\-\-console\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-quiet\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qemu-dbus-input
reads commands from standard input, one per line, and sends them to the keyboard and mouse of a
console of a QEMU started with \fB\-display dbus\fR (the interfaces org.qemu.Display1.Keyboard and
org.qemu.Display1.Mouse on /org/qemu/Display1/Console_\fIN\fR). It is the input path of a
streaming server: the server turns its client's events into these lines.
.PP
Each command is sent as soon as it is read; QEMU handles them in order. Errors (a refused call,
a malformed line) are printed as lines starting with \fBerror\fR and don't stop the program. When
standard input ends, the keys still held down are released and the program exits once QEMU has
answered every command.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBkey\fR \fICODE\fR \fB1\fR|\fB0\fR
Press (1) or release (0) a key, by its Linux input event code (linux/input-event-codes.h:
KEY_ESC 1, KEY_A 30, KEY_LEFTSHIFT 42, KEY_UP 103 ...). Codes 1 to 83 and the usual extended keys
(arrows, Home, End, Page Up/Down, Insert, Delete, right Ctrl and Alt, the Windows keys, keypad
Enter and /, Pause, SysRq, F11, F12, the volume keys) are known; others are refused (use
\fBqkey\fR).
.TP
\fBqkey\fR \fIQNUM\fR \fB1\fR|\fB0\fR
The same by QEMU's key number: the XT (set 1) make code, extended keys as 0x80 | the code after
the 0xe0 prefix.
.TP
\fBbtn\fR \fIBUTTON\fR \fB1\fR|\fB0\fR
Press or release a mouse button: \fBleft\fR, \fBmiddle\fR, \fBright\fR, \fBwheel-up\fR,
\fBwheel-down\fR, \fBside\fR, \fBextra\fR, or QEMU's number for it, 0 to 6.
.TP
\fBwheel\fR \fIN\fR
N steps of the wheel (a press and a release of wheel-up each), down if N is negative.
.TP
\fBrel\fR \fIDX\fR \fIDY\fR
Relative motion. With an absolute pointer in the guest the bridge moves its own pointer position
(clamped to the console) and sends that.
.TP
\fBabs\fR \fIX\fR \fIY\fR [\fIW\fR \fIH\fR]
Absolute position in the console's pixels, or scaled from an area of W x H (the client's window)
to the console's size. With a relative mouse in the guest the bridge sends the difference from
the previous \fBabs\fR position as relative motion; the first \fBabs\fR only sets that position.
The guest's own pointer acceleration then applies, so its pointer may drift from the client's.
.TP
.B query
Prints \fBmode absolute\fR|\fBrelative width\fR \fIW\fR \fBheight\fR \fIH\fR.
.TP
.B sync
Prints \fBsync\fR \fIN\fR once QEMU has answered the N calls sent so far (to wait for the
commands before it, or to time them).
.PP
Empty lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
.SH MOUSE MODE
QEMU says in the Mouse interface's IsAbsolute property whether the guest's pointer takes
absolute positions (a USB or virtio tablet: PVE's default \fBtablet: 1\fR) or relative motion (a
PS/2 mouse, the SPARCstation's Sun mouse). QEMU refuses absolute positions for a relative pointer
and relative motion for an absolute one; the bridge converts as described above, so a client may
send either. The bridge prints \fBmode absolute\fR or \fBmode relative\fR when it changes (the
guest's driver for the tablet starts, for instance).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-address " " \fIADDRESS\fR
The D-Bus address of the VM's display bus.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-vmid " " \fIVMID\fR
The PVE VM whose bus to use: \fIunix:path=/run/wsh-dbus-display/<vmid>.sock\fR, as the wsh-pve
qemu-server starts it for a VM with \fBdisplay=dbus\fR.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-console " " \fIN\fR
The console (default 0).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
How long to wait for QEMU on the bus (default 10).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print only errors and the answers to \fBquery\fR and \fBsync\fR; without it the bridge prints
\fBready\fR, the console's path, its mouse mode and size once connected, and mode changes.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
printf 'key 30 1\\nkey 30 0\\nabs 640 400\\nbtn left 1\\nbtn left 0\\nsync\\n' |
  qemu-dbus-input --vmid 130
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The GStreamer elements \fBqemudbussrc\fR and \fBqemudbusaudiosrc\fR (gst-inspect-1.0) in the same
package; the wsh-pve display reference, "D-Bus display".
